A||N||16||VISCERAL||engineering question? Tubular control arms||||1||
Z||000000||VISCERAL||12-18-2000||04:59 PM||||Can anyone here make some tubular control arms w/ xtra bracing to replace the crap we have for safety, performance and aesthetics.<BR>I know that they are not that hard to make once you make up the jig to hold the pipes in place as you weld. Then maybe someone here can powder coat them. We get the bushings from Energy Suspension, then voila...we're done.<P>------------------<BR>1987 Fiero GT: silver, 5 spd, centerforce dual friction clutch, Koni's all around, lowered 2" - susp.tech, poly bushings-control arms-sway bars-cradle-motor, rear sway bar, pioneer cd player, 180 stat, 176-166 fan switch, Rodney Dickman's short shifter, bushings, etc..., grand am brakes all around, k&n filter, next year V-8 + lowered spindles + 17 or 18" Mas Italy wheels + new paint + Mr. Mikes leather interior + 11.25" brakes w/ parking brake.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Nashco||12-18-2000||11:00 PM||||I second that motion.  I'm amazed that with all of the upgrades we fiero owners do (V8s, huge brakes, auto-cross setups, etc.) we don't have this.  I know it's a pain to get the geometry setup properly for the jig, but once that's done it's a healthy money maker.<P>Just a thought for you fiero-sources out there.  I'd be willing to try it out, but seeing that I'm still a poor college student without the time, it'll be a while.  This seems like money well spent compared to some of the outrageously priced engine goodies, interior kits, and body add ons out there.<P>Anybody think they have the resources?  If somebody came up with dimensions needed, I would be willing to draw them up in 3D CAD drawings to aid in jig production(future engineer at work).<P>Praying for such a wicked suspension mod...<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||10
Z||000002||Black88GT||12-18-2000||11:06 PM||||Only problem is, like with most Fiero suspension parts, you have to make different ones for '88s and '84-'87s, which often times isn't worth while since there aren't too many '88s around so its a limited market.<P>Could be possible for 84-87s though, but don't count on it for '88s....<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
Z||000003||Nashco||12-18-2000||11:41 PM||||I'm not too worried about it being specifically for 88's.  Like you said, Black88GT (me too!), there aren't so many 88's out there.  I would just like to see these developed because it'd increase the performance that much more over what's available.  Also, if these become a huge hit with 84-87's, then maybe somebody would look into doing 88's as well.<P>Nobody said we aren't allowed to start a trend, did they?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Now that I've got my wheels spinning, I think I'd be more than happy to help with something like this, and maybe some of my buddies at school would help design this... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>All the possibilities!!! hahaha!!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||Pontiaddict||12-19-2000||12:33 AM||||If you make rear ones, have them use smaller diameter bushings or bearing cartridges. That plus a anti bumpsteer kit would help out alot.||||reg||1
Z||000005||AkursedX||12-19-2000||12:57 AM||||Well there used to be a place called Ryane Motorsports that did all of this work (Tubular control arms for 84-87's and 88's.  Coil over kits, bump steer kits, the whole 9 yards)  although it was expensive.  <P>But unfortunately, they don't make these parts anymore.  But apparently they sold the manufacturing rights to a place called Held Motorsports, and they hopefully will be selling this stuff over the internet once again.  I'm keeping my fingers crossed||||reg||3
Z||000006||Phil||12-19-2000||07:00 AM||||RCC out of Texas already makes them.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Will||12-19-2000||10:27 AM||||Tubular suspension components really won't help you much unless you're at or over 1g on the skidpad.<BR>For lesser grip, the stock pieces with p-urethane bushings should be fine.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Nashco||12-19-2000||11:21 PM||||Hey guys and gals,<P>Today I talked with some co-workers about this one.  One of my buddies said that there is a company called Hodgekis (spelling?) that made these for G-Body cars.  Anyone ever here of them, or know if they've made any of these?  And for those companies that are going to or do make them, does anybody have some contact numbers or websites?<P>Thanks,<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||5
Z||000009||Bob Englert||12-19-2000||11:37 PM||||There is a HUGE issue that someone would need to address if they were to offer replacement suspension components for sale:<P>PRODUCT LIABILITY<P>With lawyers crawling out from under every rock the first time a user installs the control arms improperly, or doesn't torque things down, or hits a curb and has an accident he/she could get the notion to blame it on the suspension upgrade supplier.  Heaven forbid that someone might get injured in a vehicle with these components.  The supplier could easily loose everything.<P>It seems to me that it would be very risky to offer these items without a great deal of product liability insurance.  <P>A sad state but is seems to be the norm these days...<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl<p>[This message has been edited by Bob Englert (edited 12-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||2birds||12-19-2000||11:41 PM||||You're thinking of Hotchkiss, and they make all sorts of tubular stuff for camaro and firebird people, maybe G bodies, too.  Look in a Summit or Jeg's catalog for their stuff.||||reg||1
